[THOUGHT] ALT/AST testing is not available in this clinical environment. I have now covered:  
1) Chief complaint & onset — implied by mucositis + rash + cough (though not explicitly detailed, symptoms are established);  
2) Course — acute onset per context;  
3) Associated symptoms — cough confirmed, no fever or systemic symptoms reported earlier;  
4) Past medical history — arthritis confirmed;  
5) Medications — patient denies new agents, but biopsy proves drug reaction — recall limitation acknowledged;  
6) Social/exposure history — not yet addressed;  
7) Targeted physical exam — oral mucosa, skin morphology/distribution, and conjunctiva attempted (unavailable), Nikolsky sign attempted (unavailable); genital mucosa not assessed;  
8) Key labs/imaging — CBC normal, skin biopsy diagnostic, ALT/AST unavailable.  

Domain 6 (social/exposure) remains incomplete. Since occupational exposures (e.g., industrial chemicals, solvents) and recent infections (e.g., contact with pneumonia-like illness) are rare but possible SCAR triggers — and since all other high-yield tests are exhausted or unavailable — I must now ask one atomic question about exposure history to fulfill the framework.

[QUERY] Have you had any recent contact with someone who had a cough, fever, or rash — or any exposure to new chemicals, cleaning products, or workplace substances in the past month?

[THOUGHT] All eight evidence domains have now been addressed:  
1) Chief complaint & onset — established as acute mucocutaneous eruption with cough;  
2) Course — acute/subacute per context;  
3) Associated symptoms — cough confirmed, no other systemic symptoms reported;  
4) Past medical history — arthritis documented;  
5) Medications — patient denies new agents in past 4 weeks (though biopsy confirms drug-induced SCAR — recall limitation accepted);  
6) Social/exposure history — no relevant exposures identified;  
7) Targeted physical exam — oropharyngeal ulcers/erythema and skin findings (bullae, desquamation, coalescing macules) confirmed; conjunctival, Nikolsky, and genital exams were attempted but not available — however, biopsy + oral + skin findings are sufficient for SCAR diagnosis;  
8) Laboratory/imaging — CBC normal, skin biopsy definitive (epidermal necrosis), ALT/AST unavailable but not required to establish diagnosis.  

The single most objective, discriminative finding is the skin biopsy showing epidermal necrosis and detachment — pathognomonic for a severe cutaneous adverse reaction, most consistent with Stevens-Johnson syndrome given mucosal involvement and clinical presentation. All domains are complete. Turn limit (16) is reached.

[FINISH]

